---
// src/pages/about.astro
// The lengthier "about / methodology" page. Orients a newcomer, sets out what
// the dataset is and isn't, and explains how the numbers reconcile against the
// providers' own filings.

import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { fetchFromSupabase } from "../lib/supabase.js";

const providers = await fetchFromSupabase("provider_overview", {
  select: "provider_slug,provider_name",
  order: "provider_name.asc",
});

// De-duplicate (provider_overview is one row per filing).
const seen = new Set();
const unique = [];
for (const p of providers) {
  if (seen.has(p.provider_slug)) continue;
  seen.add(p.provider_slug);
  unique.push(p);
}

const MARKETPLACE_SLUGS = new Set([
  "amazon-store", "zalando", "aliexpress", "shein", "temu",
]);
const marketplaces = unique.filter(p => MARKETPLACE_SLUGS.has(p.provider_slug));
const social = unique.filter(p => !MARKETPLACE_SLUGS.has(p.provider_slug));
const providerCount = unique.length;

function nameList(items) {
  const names = items.map(p => p.provider_name);
  if (names.length <= 1) return names[0] ?? "";
  return `${names.slice(0, -1).join(", ")} and ${names[names.length - 1]}`;
}
const socialNames = nameList(social);
const marketplaceNames = nameList(marketplaces);
---

<Layout title="About - RTFP" description="What RTFP is, what it isn't, how the data is built, and how every number reconciles against the providers' own published filings.">
  <h2>About</h2>
  <p>
    RTFP - Read The Fine Print - is a cross-platform comparison of the
    largest platforms' EU Digital Services Act transparency reports. We load
    each platform's published filing into one normalised database and surface the
    comparisons that no one provider reports alone. It is run
    independently and is not commercial.
  </p>

  <h3>Why now</h3>
  <p>
    The DSA has required the largest platforms to publish transparency reports
    twice a year since 2023. For the first two years, each platform reported in
    its own format, with its own categories and its own definitions, so
    comparison across platforms was close to impossible. From the H2 2025
    reporting cycle - the filings published in February 2026 - every platform
    must use the harmonised template defined by Commission Implementing
    Regulation (EU) 2024/2835 of 4 November 2024. For the first time, the same
    questions are answered by every platform in the same shape, and the numbers
    can be set side by side. RTFP exists to do exactly that.
  </p>
  <p>
    Lists of where these reports live already exist - the Commission publishes
    one, and so do others. What none of them does is make the data itself
    queryable. RTFP loads the filings, normalises them, and opens the result up
    for anyone to read, compare, and check.
  </p>

  <h3>What's covered</h3>
  <p>
    The dataset currently holds the H2 2025 filings of {providerCount}
    platforms{social.length > 0 && marketplaces.length > 0 ? ", in two groups" : ""}.
  </p>
  {social.length > 0 && (
    <p><strong>Social platforms:</strong> {socialNames}.</p>
  )}
  {marketplaces.length > 0 && (
    <p><strong>Marketplaces:</strong> {marketplaceNames}.</p>
  )}
  <p>
    The "social platforms" and "marketplaces" labels are our own shorthand,
    based on general usage rather than any category in the regulation. The
    dataset will grow: this is a trial phase, and we are still working towards
    covering the filings of all the largest platforms. The next cycle, covering
    H1 2026, is due in August 2026, and any amendments to past filings will be
    added as soon as we can.
  </p>
  <p>
    ! Reader beware: "Lies, damned lies, and statistics". Data in the same style or format 
    don't mean we're all playing the same sport. Standardised data can still lie perfectly 
    in unison. For example, just because two numbers sit in the same column header 
    now doesn't mean different platforms measured them the exact same way. A spike 
    in a number on one platform might look "worse" than Platform B, but it could 
    very well be a reflection of a different user base or more aggressive internal 
    reporting and operational realities or mechanisms. Exercise statistical caution.
  </p>
  
  <h3>How the data is built</h3>
  <p>
    Each platform publishes its H2 2025 report as a bundle of CSV files laid out
    according to Annex II of the Implementing Regulation. We load those files as
    filed, preserving the original figures, and link every row back to the
    specific filing it came from - which platform, which reporting period, which
    publication date and version. Nothing is recomputed. Every number traces to a 
    cell in a published CSV.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Harmonised" turns out to mean templated rather than uniform. Platforms fill
    the same template differently: some leave whole sections blank, some invent
    sub-categories the template doesn't define, some report the same country or
    language under different codes. Where it is needed to make platforms
    comparable, we normalise these surface differences - for example, Greece is
    reported as both GR and EL across platforms, and a few platforms write a
    country code where a language code is expected. These normalisations are
    applied transparently in database views, and the underlying raw figures are
    never overwritten. Anything a platform reported that doesn't fit the
    template is recorded as a data-quality observation rather than quietly
    dropped.
  </p>

  <h3>What this is not</h3>
  <p>
    This is not an audit. Every number on the site is what a platform reported
    about itself. Where platforms disagree on methodology - and they do - that
    disagreement is surfaced as a finding, not resolved. Garbage in, garbage
    out: if a platform's filing is wrong, RTFP will faithfully show you the
    wrong number, traced back to where it came from.
  </p>
  <p>
    RTFP does not include the EU's Statements of Reasons database, which is a
    separate dataset covering individual moderation decisions. It covers the
    quantitative templates filed under Articles 15 and 24 of the DSA, and the
    qualitative narrative sections under Article 42.
  </p>
  <p>
    RTFP only loads the csv files corresponding to #1 to #11. Some platforms publish 
    a conslidated csv (or spreadsheet), and others also include separate reporting in prose, 
    such as a written report. We do not include those other files here - for now.
  </p>

  <h3>How the numbers reconcile</h3>
  <p>
    Every finding links to the database view behind it. Every view is publicly
    queryable - without writing any code on the <a href="/explore">explore</a>
    page, or directly via the <a href="/api">API</a>. And every filing the
    site draws from is listed on the <a href="/sources">sources</a> page, linked
    to the provider's own published report, so any number here can be checked
    against the original. If a figure on RTFP ever disagrees with a provider's
    filing, the provider's filing wins - please tell us and we'll correct it.
  </p>

  <h3>Finding your way around</h3>
  <p>
    The <a href="/findings">findings</a> are the comparisons worth knowing. The
    <a href="/data">platforms</a> pages show the full filing for any one
    platform. The <a href="/explore">explore</a> page lets you query the whole
    database yourself. <a href="/sources">Sources</a> traces everything back to
    the originals, and the <a href="/api">API</a> is for anyone who wants the
    data as data.
  </p>

  <h3>Who built this</h3>
  <p>
    RTFP is built by Oscar (<a href="https://github.com/pushkinoxt">@pushkinoxt</a>)
    and Sune.
  </p>

  <slot name="footer" slot="footer">
    <p>An independent, non-commercial comparison built from platforms' self-reported DSA filings. Not an audit.</p>
    <p>Found an error? <a href="https://github.com/pushkinoxt/rtfp/issues">Open an issue</a>.</p>
  </slot>
</Layout>